  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n~1019 mb surface high pressure is centered over northern Indiana \nearly this morning, with surface ridging extending westward into the \nMiddle Mississippi Valley. Light winds and mostly clear skies have \nallowed temperatures to drop into the upper 50s to low 60s this \nmorning. Tranquil weather is expected to continue through today, \nwith high temperatures topping out in the upper 70s to low 80s. \nIncreasing cloud cover ahead of an approaching cold front will keep \ntemperatures a little warmer tonight, in the low to middle 60s.\n\nA mid-level shortwave will track through the southern Canadian \nPrairies and into the northern Great Lakes Region on Wednesday, \nsending a cold front through Illinois. Out ahead of the front, \ntemperatures will climb well into the 80s with low 70s dewpoints \nexpected to pool ahead of the front. By mid to late afternoon, CAMs \nshow a broken line of supercell thunderstorms developing in a \nmoderately unstable airmass with SBCAPE up around 2000-2500 J/kg and \n30 kts of wind shear. Although the better forcing will be displaced \nto our north closer to the shortwave, a few strong to severe storms \ncould develop from late Wednesday afternoon and evening, posing a \nthreat for damaging wind gusts and isolated large hail. Modest \ndirectional shear noted in forecast hodographs may be enough to \nsupport a brief tornado or two.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft remains in place through late this week, which \nwill ultimately keep things relatively dry and pleasant with \ntemperatures near our seasonal normals. Upper ridging will begin \nshifting east this weekend, raising mid-level heights locally and \nbringing the return of summer-like heat. Temperatures look to climb \ninto the upper 80s to low 90s, with the NBM indicating favorable \nprobabilities (40-60%+) for temperatures over 90 degrees south of a \nSpringfield to Effingham line on Sunday and a tier or two of \ncounties north of there on Monday. Precipitation chances late this \nweekend into early next week look to remain confined over the \nnorthern half of the state with the upper ridge preventing any \nfrontal systems from intruding too far south.",
